        <!-- SECTION 8 -->
        <h2 id="tips">8. What Actually Makes This Work</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Agree the wheel is final before the first spin.</strong>
            State it explicitly: whatever it picks, the team honours it, no
            re-spinning because someone didn't like the result. Without this
            norm stated upfront, the tool loses its value the first time someone
            asks for a do-over.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Keep the sound on during the screen-share.</strong> The
            ticking as it slows down is most of what makes a spin feel like a
            small event rather than a utility. Check once that your platform
            passes desktop audio through, then leave it on.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong
              >Use "remove after spin" for anything rotation-based.</strong
            >
            Speaking order, role assignment, prize draws — this keeps the same
            person from being picked twice and makes the distribution provably
            fair over time.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Drop a shared link for async calls.</strong> For decisions
            that don't need a live meeting — like picking next month's social
            format — post the link in Slack: "first person to spin decides."
            Fast, playful, no meeting required.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Add a wildcard entry to activity wheels.</strong> One entry
            labelled "team pick" gives the group a rare moment of collective
            choice if it lands, without giving up the structure the rest of the
            wheel provides.
          </li>
        </ul>

